= Scaling — Technical =

'''Scaling''' is the dependence of a system's performance, function, cost or quality on a change in its resource budget, problem size, workload or physical dimensions. A scientifically meaningful scaling claim is conditional on an explicitly measured output, controlled change, operating regime and comparison baseline. It is useful across parallel computing, distributed services, physical similarity, engineering production and statistical models, but no single scaling exponent or "law" automatically covers all of these.

This article distinguishes '''strong scaling''' (fixed total problem size while increasing resources), '''weak scaling''' (increasing problem size roughly in proportion to resources), '''capacity scaling''' (increasing offered load while preserving defined service constraints), '''cost scaling''' (resource and economic burden), and '''physical/geometric scaling''' (changes in characteristic size under mechanical and dimensional constraints). It examines tractability, throughput, latency, bottlenecks, saturation and nonideal parallelism with independent analytic examples.

== Define the independent and dependent variables ==

Let <math>N</math> denote workload or problem size, <math>p</math> available processing resources and <math>\theta</math> the architecture, data distribution and environment. Let <math>T(N,p;\theta)</math> be completion time for a well-defined job, <math>X(\lambda,p;\theta)</math> sustained throughput at offered arrival rate <math>\lambda</math>, and <math>C(N,p;\theta)</math> operating cost. These are different functions; changing <math>N</math> and changing <math>p</math> are different experiments.

For a fixed workload <math>N_0</math>, the '''speedup''' and '''parallel efficiency''' are conventionally

<math display="block">S_p(N_0)=\frac{T(N_0,1)}{T(N_0,p)},\qquad E_p(N_0)=\frac{S_p(N_0)}{p}.</math>

The reference must represent a specified one-resource execution on comparable hardware. Different baselines, memory capacity, cache effects and optimized serial algorithms can alter reported speedup. Superlinear measured speedup is possible, for instance from improved cache locality or memory behavior; it does not invalidate the definition, but it means a simplistic fixed-fraction model is inappropriate.

Under a weak-scaling experiment, set <math>N(p)=pN_0</math> and compare <math>T(pN_0,p)</math> with <math>T(N_0,1)</math>. Idealized weak-scaling efficiency may be expressed as

<math display="block">E_{\mathrm{weak}}(p)=\frac{T(N_0,1)}{T(pN_0,p)}</math>

under a fixed per-resource workload convention. Many real studies vary time horizon or use a different weak-scaling metric, so reproducibility requires the exact formula, benchmark problem and tolerances.<ref name="gustafson">John L. Gustafson, [https://doi.org/10.1145/42411.42415 “Reevaluating Amdahl's Law”], ''Communications of the ACM'' 31(5), 532–533 (1988). Contrast between fixed-problem and scaled-problem interpretations.</ref>

== Amdahl's fixed-workload ceiling ==

Suppose a fixed program spends fraction <math>s\in[0,1]</math> of its one-processor time in work that receives no speedup and fraction <math>1-s</math> in perfectly divisible parallel work. Under homogeneous processors, no communication overhead and identical work, its normalized completion time is

<math display="block">\frac{T_p}{T_1}=s+\frac{1-s}{p},\qquad S_p=\frac{1}{s+(1-s)/p}.</math>

For <math>s>0</math>, <math>\lim_{p\to\infty}S_p=1/s</math>. This is a limit of the specified fixed-fraction idealization, not a universal ceiling of every parallel program. Amdahl's original 1967 argument focused on the limits imposed by essential serial execution in large computing systems.<ref name="amdahl">Gene M. Amdahl, [https://doi.org/10.1145/1465482.1465560 “Validity of the Single Processor Approach to Achieving Large Scale Computing Capabilities”], ''Proceedings of the AFIPS Spring Joint Computer Conference'', 483–485 (1967). Primary historical statement.</ref>

For <math>s=0.1</math> and <math>p=8</math>,

<math display="block">S_8=\frac{1}{0.1+0.9/8}=\frac{80}{17}\approx4.70588,\qquad E_8=\frac{10}{17}\approx0.588235.</math>

Even an arbitrarily large <math>p</math> cannot exceed a 10-fold speedup in this precise fixed-workload model. Its 8-processor efficiency of about 58.8% is a '''model result''', not measured performance of a particular machine.

A more realistic decomposition includes communication, scheduling and resource contention:

<math display="block">T_p=T_{\mathrm{serial}}+\frac{T_{\mathrm{parallel}}}{p}+T_{\mathrm{sync}}(p)+T_{\mathrm{comm}}(p)+T_{\mathrm{memory}}(p).</math>

These terms are not always separable additive quantities; overlap among computation, memory and communication must be modeled when relevant. The original <math>s</math> may also change with workload, algorithm and processor configuration. Observed performance that differs from the toy formula does not establish that the hardware is defective.

=== Gustafson's scaled-size interpretation ===

Suppose serial work accounts for fraction <math>\alpha</math> of the measured parallel execution time on <math>p</math> processors, while the parallelizable fraction can be done <math>p</math> times faster than its corresponding serial work on the same scaled problem. Under those stated assumptions, Gustafson's scaled speedup is

<math display="block">S_{\mathrm{scaled}}(p)=\alpha+p(1-\alpha)=p-\alpha(p-1).</math>

For <math>\alpha=0.1</math>, <math>p=8</math> gives <math>S_{\mathrm{scaled}}=8-0.1\cdot7=7.3</math>. One must not set <math>\alpha=s</math> automatically: Amdahl's <math>s</math> is measured relative to the serial fixed-problem time, while the scaled fraction in Gustafson's derivation refers to a different workload and execution-time normalization.<ref name="gustafson"/>

The two models answer distinct questions rather than supplying competing universally true forecasts. If the scientific problem grows, parallelism can offer substantial useful capacity without making a fixed-size problem proportionally faster.

== Queueing, throughput and latency ==

In an online or physical production service, distinguish the offered arrival rate <math>\lambda</math>, the actual completed-job throughput <math>X</math>, the mean number of jobs in the defined system <math>L</math>, and mean sojourn time <math>W</math>. Under suitable steady long-run averages and a consistent boundary, Little's relation is

<math display="block">L=\lambda_{\mathrm{eff}}W,</math>

where <math>\lambda_{\mathrm{eff}}</math> is the effective long-run rate through that boundary, not necessarily the unconstrained offered rate when requests are rejected or lost. The relation itself is broad, but applying it to a measurement requires a stable regime and consistent job definitions.<ref name="little">John D. C. Little, [https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.9.3.383 “A Proof for the Queuing Formula: L = λW”], ''Operations Research'' 9(3), 383–387 (1961), original proof.</ref>

For example, if a stable service completes 80 jobs/s and mean time in system is 0.25 s, the expected mean number in system is <math>L=80\cdot0.25=20</math> jobs. This is a consistency equation; it does not by itself give a distribution of queue lengths or determine the architecture and processing speed.

=== Saturation in a stylized M/M/1 queue ===

For an ideal infinite-buffer single-server queue with Poisson arrivals of rate <math>\lambda</math>, independent exponential service times of rate <math>\mu</math>, and stable <math>\lambda<\mu</math>, the steady-state utilization and mean time in system are

<math display="block">\rho=\frac{\lambda}{\mu},\qquad W=\frac{1}{\mu-\lambda},\qquad L=\lambda W=\frac{\lambda}{\mu-\lambda}.</math>

For <math>\mu=100</math> jobs/s and <math>\lambda=80</math> jobs/s, utilization is 0.8, <math>W=0.05</math> s and <math>L=4</math> jobs. When <math>\lambda</math> rises to 90 jobs/s without a service-rate change, utilization rises to 0.9, <math>W=0.10</math> s and <math>L=9</math> jobs. This is a twofold latency increase for a 12.5% increase in offered work, '''under the M/M/1 assumptions'''. It shows why near-saturation delay can rise nonlinearly without a proportional resource increase.

